---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Salons"
  description="A monthly salon in Los Angeles, and an invite-only online series for founding donors."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Salons</h1>

    <h2>The Los Angeles salon</h2>
    <p>
      Once a month, one topic, in person: an evening built for genuine
      disagreement across the spectrum — the same bar the amendments have to
      pass. Every topic is a real open question where the discussion changes a
      decision, not a briefing.
    </p>

    <h3>Up next</h3>
    <div class="topic">
      <p class="topic-title">
        What should a privacy amendment actually protect?
      </p>
      <p>
        Should a constitutional right to privacy cover your <em>personal
        information</em> — data, communications, surveillance — or the broader
        <em>right to be let alone</em>? The state amendments that won landslide
        votes took the first path; the second reaches further but carries far
        more doctrinal freight. This is likely the single most consequential
        open decision in the privacy category, and it isn't settled.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="placeholder-note">
      RSVP opens with the first scheduled date — via Luma, cross-listed on
      Eventbrite.
    </p>

    <h2>The founding donors' series</h2>
    <p>
      A separate, invite-only online salon series is reserved for founding
      donors. <a href="/get-involved/seed-donors">Learn about becoming one →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .topic {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .topic-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
